owel sound, but the next word begins with a consonant sound. Choose by pronunciation, not just spelling.","B":"The would identify a particular item, but the prompt explicitly introduces one nonspecific item.","C":"A singular countable noun needs a determiner here; leaving the article out does not form the requested phrase.","D":"Use a for one nonspecific useful tool. The first sound of “useful tool” is a consonant sound."}}},{"id":"b7b818fd-d132-55db-ace6-863760da128b","topicSlug":"articles","level":"A1","prompt":"I found a useful tool. ____ useful tool I found belongs to my neighbour.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A"},{"id":"B","text":"An"},{"id":"C","text":"—"},{"id":"D","text":"The"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound. Focus on **second mention** in this question.","explanation":"The second mention identifies the same useful tool, so use the.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"second-mention","contextKey":"noun-useful-tool","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-articles","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"776eaeea825460bd6e57e69a2753afe9e1ceb0019e0850b286d26952747870a0","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **second mention**. Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound.","The second mention identifies the same useful tool, so use the.","Choose **The**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**A** would introduce another nonspecific item; this mention identifies the same item already introduced.","B":"**An** would introduce another nonspecific item; this mention identifies the same item already introduced.","C":"No article would leave a general or incomplete reference; this question explicitly identifies a particular item or set.","D":"The second mention identifies the same useful tool, so use the."}}},{"id":"6cd67449-a7e1-5bc7-ac72-b2ef4e204967","topicSlug":"articles","level":"A1","prompt":"Talk about the whole category, not particular items: ____ useful tools come in many varieties.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"—"},{"id":"B","text":"a"},{"id":"C","text":"an"},{"id":"D","text":"the"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound. Focus on **general plural** in this question.","explanation":"For useful tools in general, use the plural noun without an article. — means no article.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"general-plural","contextKey":"noun-useful-tool","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-articles","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"6a6fd9bf2dcfba7be39c0ee1bb465b98582685d8b6e081cdf73fe6061eaff17d","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **general plural**. Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound.","For useful tools in general, use the plural noun without an article. — means no article.","Choose **—**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"For useful tools in general, use the plural noun without an article. — means no article.","B":"**a** is singular, but the noun in this question is plural.","C":"**an** is singular, but the noun in this question is plural.","D":"The would narrow the reference to an identifiable set, but the prompt asks about the whole category."}}},{"id":"db026b17-b489-5a53-a312-6df997648fe9","topicSlug":"articles","level":"A1","prompt":"We are discussing a particular set: please count ____ useful tools in this photograph.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"a"},{"id":"B","text":"an"},{"id":"C","text":"—"},{"id":"D","text":"the"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound. Focus on **identified plural** in this question.","explanation":"The phrase “in this photograph” identifies a specific set of useful tools; use the.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"identified-plural","contextKey":"noun-useful-tool","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-articles","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"88aba8fdb918ca597fe6235f42841adf0125fbe47be1a8b774042e4d872c3e2f","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **identified plural**. Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound.","The phrase “in this photograph” identifies a specific set of useful tools; use the.","Choose **the**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**a** is singular, but the noun in this question is plural.","B":"**an** is singular, but the noun in this question is plural.","C":"No article would leave a general or incomplete reference; this question explicitly identifies a particular item or set.","D":"The phrase “in this photograph” identifies a specific set of useful tools; use the."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Articles help the listener identify what a noun refers to. A/an introduces one member of a class; the signals that the referent is identifiable; no article often gives a general plural or uncountable meaning. Start with countability and number, then ask whether the listener can identify the thing."],"rules":[{"title":"One nonspecific item","explanation":"Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is not yet identified. Choose by the next sound: an before a vowel sound and a before a consonant sound.","examples":["a uniform","an hourglass","She found **an** old coin. **The** coin was silver."],"pattern":"**a** uniform · **an** hour","paragraphs":["Choose a or an from the next sound, not simply the next letter. A university starts with a /j/ sound, so it takes a. An hour starts with a vowel sound because h is silent. An adjective can determine the article: a box but an empty box."]},{"title":"An identified item","explanation":"Use the when the listener can identify the item, including a second mention. The also works with identified plural nouns.","examples":["I found a ring. The ring was silver.","Please return the borrowed books.","**Water** freezes, but **the water in this bottle** is warm."],"pattern":"First mention: **a** key → identified again: **the** key","paragraphs":["The does not always require an earlier mention. A shared situation can identify the referent: Please close the door. A defining phrase can also identify it: the keys on the table. The works with singular, plural and uncountable nouns when the intended reference is identifiable."]},{"title":"General plural nouns","explanation":"Usually omit the article before a plural noun when referring to its whole class. In these exercises, — means no article.","examples":["Bicycles need regular maintenance.","The bicycles outside are ours.","He is **a** teacher at **the** school near our house."],"pattern":"**Books** can be expensive. / **The books on this shelf** are mine.","paragraphs":["General plural nouns normally have no article: Dogs need exercise. General uncountable nouns also often have no article: Music helps me relax. A singular count noun normally needs a determiner: I bought a book, not I bought book."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Names and fixed institutional expressions require extra learning. Most country and city names have no article, but rivers, seas and ranges commonly take the. In British English, at school can describe attendance, while at the school can identify the site. American in the hospital is normal usage.","examples":["She found **an** old coin. **The** coin was silver.","**Water** freezes, but **the water in this bottle** is warm.","He is **a** teacher at **the** school near our house."]}],"recap":["**a** uniform · **an** hour","First mention: **a** key → identified again: **the** key","**Books** can be expensive. / **The books on this shelf** are mine."]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["demonstratives"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
